{
  "$comment": "Canonical cross-engine operator contract for RuleMind. Every evaluation engine (TypeScript packages/rule-engine, Python apps/python-executor, Kotlin sdk-android/rulemind-core, Dart sdk-flutter) MUST satisfy every case below. Conformance tests in each language load this file and assert their compare() returns `expected`. Fixtures use native JSON types and set fieldType on ordering/equality cases so all engines agree without relying on value-string-parsing differences. Do not weaken a case to make an engine pass \u2014 fix the engine. CONTRACT by fieldType: (1) fieldType 'date' \u2014 both sides are normalized to a UTC epoch via a strict ISO-8601 regex + integer civil-days math (identical in every engine, NOT a native date parser), so dates are truly ORDERED (>,>=,<,<=,between) and equality is spelling-insensitive ('2026-01-01' == '2026-1-1'); a non-ISO/out-of-range value \u2192 false for every operator. (2) fieldType 'boolean' \u2014 ==/!= on coerced booleans. (3) all other ordered ops (>,>=,<,<=,between) are NUMERIC-ONLY \u2014 a non-numeric operand (e.g. a plain string) makes it false; strings are never lexically ordered. (4) == / != are LOOSE/numeric-aware everywhere, so '750' == 750 (Python used strict == before and diverged \u2014 now fixed). in/not_in also use loose equality; regex/exists/!exists are string/presence checks.",
